1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,214
A coding assistant that turns a described workflow into a working script, a tool

2
00:00:05,214 --> 00:00:10,260
like ChatPRD that turns a rough idea into a structured draft, a Workspace or Notion

3
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:15,306
automation that used to need an engineer's help: all of this genuinely changes what

4
00:00:15,306 --> 00:00:19,198
one PM can build alone, this afternoon, without filing a ticket.

5
00:00:19,198 --> 00:00:22,967
That's real, and this course isn't going to pretend otherwise.

6
00:00:22,967 --> 00:00:27,953
What it won't do is stop there, because the interesting question was never whether

7
00:00:27,953 --> 00:00:28,986
you can build it.

8
00:00:28,986 --> 00:00:33,972
It's what happens six weeks later, when the small script you wrote for yourself is

9
00:00:33,972 --> 00:00:39,079
quietly the thing three other people now depend on, and nobody ever asked whether it

10
00:00:39,079 --> 00:00:40,538
was built to carry that.

11
00:00:40,538 --> 00:00:43,578
None of this is really new in kind, only in speed.

12
00:00:43,578 --> 00:00:48,503
PMs have always found ways to work around a full engineering queue: a spreadsheet

13
00:00:48,503 --> 00:00:52,090
formula, a macro, a borrowed hour from a friendly engineer.

14
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:56,832
What changed is that the gap between having an idea and having a working thing

15
00:00:56,832 --> 00:01:01,757
collapsed from days to minutes, and a gap that used to force you to slow down and

16
00:01:01,757 --> 00:01:04,189
think no longer does that automatically.

17
00:01:04,739 --> 00:01:09,727
Read the blast radius column the exact same way 6.6 taught you to read it for an

18
00:01:09,727 --> 00:01:14,280
agent's actions: can this be undone, and what does it cost if it's wrong.

19
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:16,836
Vibe-coding doesn't change that question.

20
00:01:16,836 --> 00:01:21,451
It only changes how fast you can build something before anyone's asked it.

21
00:01:21,451 --> 00:01:25,130
The top two rows are where this skill earns its reputation.

22
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:30,119
A script that summarises your own inbox, a rough prototype you'll throw away the

23
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:35,294
moment the real PRD conversation starts, cost nothing if they're wrong, because the

24
00:01:35,294 --> 00:01:38,973
only person affected is you, and you'll notice immediately.

25
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:40,408
Build these constantly.

26
00:01:40,408 --> 00:01:44,149
This is the fastest feedback loop available to a working PM.

27
00:01:44,149 --> 00:01:49,138
The bottom two rows are where the exact same skill gets genuinely dangerous, and

28
00:01:49,138 --> 00:01:54,314
it's dangerous for a reason specific to vibe-coding: the tool got built fast enough

29
00:01:54,314 --> 00:01:59,489
that none of this course's earlier discipline, a golden set, a risk register row, a

30
00:01:59,489 --> 00:02:02,982
named owner, ever had a natural moment to attach itself.

31
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:07,409
Slow, traditional engineering forced those checkpoints by taking weeks.

32
00:02:07,409 --> 00:02:11,649
This doesn't, which means you have to add them back in deliberately.

33
00:02:12,199 --> 00:02:16,949
The last row deserves the most caution, because it's the one most likely to feel

34
00:02:16,949 --> 00:02:19,918
like the first row while actually being the third.

35
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:24,906
Would you still call it harmless if it were a spreadsheet of customer emails instead

36
00:02:24,906 --> 00:02:25,975
of your own inbox?

37
00:02:25,975 --> 00:02:30,903
A prototype built to prove an idea and a tool that now processes real customer data

38
00:02:30,903 --> 00:02:35,890
are not the same category, even when they started from the identical five minutes of

39
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:36,306
typing.

40
00:02:36,856 --> 00:02:41,625
Notice the trigger in the good column: a second person depending on it, not a

41
00:02:41,625 --> 00:02:43,731
calendar date or a size threshold.

42
00:02:43,731 --> 00:02:48,747
That's the actual moment a personal tool becomes a small piece of infrastructure,

43
00:02:48,747 --> 00:02:53,516
and it's a moment worth noticing on purpose rather than discovering during an

44
00:02:53,516 --> 00:02:54,074
incident.

45
00:02:54,074 --> 00:02:59,152
This is 8.6's incident-response lecture in miniature, arriving earlier than anyone

46
00:02:59,152 --> 00:02:59,710
expected.

47
00:02:59,710 --> 00:03:04,664
A tool with no named owner and no documented failure mode is exactly the gap 8.6

48
00:03:04,664 --> 00:03:09,867
warned about, just wearing a smaller, friendlier shape because you built it yourself

49
00:03:09,867 --> 00:03:13,335
in an afternoon instead of commissioning it from a team.

50
00:03:13,335 --> 00:03:16,741
Ask the ownership question specifically, not generally.

51
00:03:16,741 --> 00:03:21,944
"Someone should probably own this eventually" is the sentence that lets a tool drift

52
00:03:21,944 --> 00:03:23,988
for months with no name attached.

53
00:03:23,988 --> 00:03:29,128
"I own this until a specific date, then we decide together whether it graduates" is

54
00:03:29,128 --> 00:03:34,331
a sentence with an actual deadline built into it, and deadlines are what turn a good

55
00:03:34,331 --> 00:03:36,684
intention into something that happens.

56
00:03:37,234 --> 00:03:42,024
Say the honest caveat, because the entire appeal of this skill, speed, is also

57
00:03:42,024 --> 00:03:45,463
exactly what makes it easy to outrun your own judgement.

58
00:03:45,463 --> 00:03:50,438
Traditional engineering used to impose discipline by accident: a script that took

59
00:03:50,438 --> 00:03:55,535
three weeks to build got a code review, a second pair of eyes, simply because three

60
00:03:55,535 --> 00:03:57,500
weeks felt like it deserved one.

61
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:01,676
A script built in twenty minutes doesn't get that for free any more.

62
00:04:01,676 --> 00:04:06,589
You have to choose to apply the same discipline deliberately, at a speed that no

63
00:04:06,589 --> 00:04:08,063
longer forces it on you.

64
00:04:08,063 --> 00:04:12,792
This is worth saying plainly because the framing around these tools, in every

65
00:04:12,792 --> 00:04:16,845
product demo and every announcement, is speed as the entire pitch.

66
00:04:16,845 --> 00:04:18,749
Speed is real and worth having.

67
00:04:18,749 --> 00:04:23,785
It was just never the same thing as correctness, and conflating the two is exactly

68
00:04:23,785 --> 00:04:28,637
how a confidently wrong five-minute script does as much damage as a confidently

69
00:04:28,637 --> 00:04:33,734
wrong five-week project, just more often, because building one got so much cheaper.

70
00:04:34,284 --> 00:04:38,804
That's the line worth drawing before the next script, not after someone else is

71
00:04:38,804 --> 00:04:41,436
already relying on one you built for yourself.

72
00:04:41,436 --> 00:04:46,243
Most people running this list for the first time find at least one tool that quietly

73
00:04:46,243 --> 00:04:50,935
crossed the line months ago, still carrying none of the ownership or documentation

74
00:04:50,935 --> 00:04:52,937
the crossing should have triggered.

75
00:04:52,937 --> 00:04:54,311
That's not a confession.

76
00:04:54,311 --> 00:04:58,945
It's this lecture's actual finding, and it's a cheap one to fix today compared to

77
00:04:58,945 --> 00:04:59,918
during an outage.

78
00:04:59,918 --> 00:05:03,809
Most people are also surprised by their own list in both directions.

79
00:05:03,809 --> 00:05:08,215
There are usually more vibe-coded tools quietly running than anyone remembers

80
00:05:08,215 --> 00:05:12,563
building, and at least one of them is doing something with real consequences

81
00:05:12,563 --> 00:05:15,195
nobody's checked on since the week it shipped.

82
00:05:15,745 --> 00:05:17,502
One thing before you move on.

83
00:05:17,502 --> 00:05:22,047
Make the list honestly, including the small ones you'd forgotten you built.

84
00:05:22,047 --> 00:05:24,955
Most people have more of these than they expect.

85
00:05:24,955 --> 00:05:29,742
Then, for anything a teammate now depends on, give it a real owner and a row in

86
00:05:29,742 --> 00:05:34,710
8.1's register, the same treatment as any other tool this course has taught you to

87
00:05:34,710 --> 00:05:35,619
take seriously.

88
00:05:35,619 --> 00:05:39,376
Neither step needs permission from anyone else to start today.

89
00:05:39,376 --> 00:05:44,466
The list is yours to make honestly, and naming an owner for a tool you already built

90
00:05:44,466 --> 00:05:47,192
is a five-minute conversation, not a project.

91
00:05:47,192 --> 00:05:52,282
Next lecture is packaging your artifacts into a portfolio: turning everything you've

92
00:05:52,282 --> 00:05:56,948
built across this entire course, including today's list, into something you'd

93
00:05:56,948 --> 00:05:58,705
actually hand an interviewer.
